n-mission/","t":1789435982000,"lead":"The results are in: NASA\u2019s Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope\u2019s very accurate first mid-course correction, along with other fuel savings, are expected to more than double the mission\u2019s potential operational lifetime."},{"title":"NASA Roman\u2019s Planet Imager Has Powered On","link":"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/roman/2026/09/01/nasa-romans-planet-imager-has-powered-on/","t":1788282373000,"lead":"NASA\u2019s Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope team has successfully activated the mission\u2019s Coronagraph Instrument, which will block starlight to view planets and dusty disks around nearby stars."},{"title":"NASA Roman Space Telescope\u2019s Antenna, \u2018Visor\u2019 Deployed","link":"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/roman/2026/09/01/nasa-roman-space-telescopes-antenna-visor-deployed/","t":1788281975000,"lead":"Roman\u2019s antenna and visor-like sunshade have successfully deployed."},{"title":"NASA\u2019s Roman Completes First Mid-Course Correction Burn","link":"https://science.nasa.gov/blogs/roman/2026/08/31/nasas-roman-completes-first-mid-course-correction-burn/","t":1788219186000,"lead":"At 12:02 p.m."}]},"hubble_obs":{"n_obs":32,"n_targets":10,"newest":1790173604867,"items":[{"target":"MASCARA-4","inst":"STIS/CCD","t":1790173604867,"title":"Beyond hydrogen and helium: an exhaustive survey of escaping metals in ultra-hot Jupiters around bright, hot stars","cls":"EXT-STAR;A0-A3 V-IV;EXTRA-SOLAR PLANETARY SYSTEM","prop":"18108","public":true},{"target":"CCDFLAT","inst":"STIS/CCD","t":1790170703812,"title":"Rocky Worlds DDT: HST Observations of HD 260655","cls":"","prop":"18236","public":false},{"target":"HD-260655","inst":"STIS/CCD","t":1790170468836,"title":"Rocky Worlds DDT: HST Observations of HD 260655","cls":"STAR;EXTRA-SOLAR PLANETARY SYSTEM;M V-IV","prop":"18236","public":false},{"target":"ACT-CLJ0123.5-0428","inst":"WFC3/IR","t":1790156015472,"title":"Multiwavelength Observations of the Highest-Redshift Dynamically Relaxed Galaxy Cluster","cls":"CLUSTER OF GALAXIES;HIGH REDSHIFT CLUSTER","prop":"18191","public":false},{"target":"PIE-FIELD-10","inst":"WFC3/UVIS","t":1790135454979,"title":"Efficient selection of Lyman Continuum emitters at z > 3","cls":"UNIDENTIFIED;PARALLEL FIELD","prop":"18126","public":false},{"target":"SDSSJ080315.70+483603.0","inst":"COS/FUV","t":1790123626490,"title":"A clean view of the CGM for well-characterized galaxies","cls":"GALAXY;QSO","prop":"17734","public":true},{"target":"WD0308-565","inst":"COS/FUV","t":1790112830490,"title":"Cycle 33 COS FUV Spectroscopic Sensitivity Monitor","cls":"STAR;DB","prop":"18199","public":true},{"target":"PIE-FIELD-54","inst":"WFC3/UVIS","t":1790104712964,"title":"","cls":"UNIDENTIFIED;PARALLEL FIELD","prop":"18126","public":false},{"target":"WDFS0122-30","inst":"STIS/CCD","t":1790099215812,"title":"GoldSpec: Absolute Flux Calibration Stars for the Hubble/Webb/Roman Multi-Mission Era","cls":"CALIBRATION;PHOTOMETRIC","prop":"18229","public":true},{"target":"WG-7","inst":"COS/FUV","t":1790077447353,"title":"COS FUV LP11/12 Calibration: Profiles, Traces, Sensitivities, Flat Fields, and Spatial Resolution","cls":"STAR;WDO","prop":"18472","public":true}]},"webb_obs":{"n_obs":322,"n_targets":7,"newest":1790139828750,"items":[{"target":"20260714_m7_MPT_catalog.v3","inst":"NIRSPEC/MSA","t":1790139828750,"title":"Groundbreaking Constraints on the Galactic Dust Extinction Law in the High-Opacity Regime","cls":"","prop":"6927","public":false},{"target":"MAXI1750","inst":"MIRI/IFU","t":1790126667195,"title":"Legacy Observations of BHXRBs: wind driving mechanism and relation with the jet","cls":"Star; Black holes; Low-mass X-ray binary stars; X-ray binary stars","prop":"6561","public":false},{"target":"BD+60-1753","inst":"NIRISS/IMAGE","t":1790114379837,"title":"CAL-XCAL-504: Absolute Flux Calibration (Monitoring)","cls":"Star; A dwarfs","prop":"12525","public":true},{"target":"3014","inst":"MIRI/IMAGE","t":1790080602348,"title":"Moving Target Shadow MIRI","cls":"Solar System; Asteroid","prop":"12772","public":true},{"target":"LAWD-79","inst":"MIRI/IMAGE","t":1790069479751,"title":"The Fate of Jovian Worlds","cls":"Star; White dwarfs","prop":"9579","public":false},{"target":"HD-167855B","inst":"MIRI/CORON","t":1790066218856,"title":"F560W boresight offset confirmation","cls":"Star; G stars","prop":"12596","public":true},{"target":"HD-167855A","inst":"MIRI/CORON","t":1790063662955,"title":"F560W boresight offset confirmation","cls":"Star; G stars","prop":"12596","public":true}]},"aims":{"HST:18108":{"cat":"GO","aim":"While hot Jupiters orbiting A-type stars are under no threat of losing significant fractions of their mass, they are the best targets to study atmospheric escape because they have the strongest and most detectable signals of exospheric metals in transmission spectroscopy.","page":"https://www.stsci.edu/hst-program-info/program/?program=18108"},"HST:18236":{"cat":"GO/DD","aim":"We will use COS and STIS to measure the UV to blue-optical spectra of these stars, including UV flare monitoring and measuring the wings of the Ly-alpha profile where appropriate.","page":"https://www.stsci.edu/hst-program-info/program/?program=18236"},"HST:18191":{"cat":"GO","aim":"We propose joint Chandra, XMM-Newton, and HST observations of ACT-CL J0123.5-0428, a z=1.5 galaxy cluster newly identified as the highest redshift relaxed cool-core cluster known.","page":"https://www.stsci.edu/hst-program-info/program/?program=18191"},"HST:18126":{"cat":"GO","aim":"The Parallel Ionizing Emissivity (PIE) survey is an ongoing effort to address this by detecting over 400 galaxies across ~50 independent fields that can be stacked to measure how LyC emission varies with galaxy properties.","page":"https://www.stsci.edu/hst-program-info/program/?program=18126"},"HST:17734":{"cat":"GO","aim":"The sightlines cover a range of azimuths and are at low impact parameters, where the detection rate of Lya is >80% and that of metals >50%, allowing us to characterize the azimuthal and radial variations of the velocities, column density, volume density, metallicity, and linewidths, using absorption lines of hydrogen (HI), low-ions (CII/ SiII) and intermediate ions (SiIII).","page":"https://www.stsci.edu/hst-program-info/program/?program=17734"},"HST:18199":{"cat":"CAL/COS","aim":"This calibration proposal is to monitor the sensitivity of each FUV grating mode at several cenwave settings on an approximately bi-monthly schedule, and to characterize the observed trends.","page":"https://www.stsci.edu/hst-program-info/program/?program=18199"},"HST:18229":{"cat":"CAL/CROSS","aim":"The Calspec database of stellar flux standards suffers from a sufficient supply of well verified stars, especially at the faint end of the collection where the requirements of JWST and Roman predominate.","page":"https://www.stsci.edu/hst-program-info/program/?program=18229"},"HST:18472":{"cat":"CAL/COS","aim":"The data will be used to determine the 2D cross-dispersion spectral profiles and traces required to perform two-zone extraction, as well as the sensitivities and flat-fields for accurate flux calibration.","page":"https://www.stsci.edu/hst-program-info/program/?program=18472"},"JWST:6927":{"cat":"GO","aim":"To help remedy this situation, we seek NIRSpec MSA spectra prioritizing highly obscured stars in a well-known infrared dark cloud, the Brick.","page":"https://www.stsci.edu/jwst/science-execution/program-information?id=6927"},"JWST:6561":{"cat":"GO","aim":"We propose to answer this question with simultaneous time-resolved spectroscopy of a transient BHXRB in X-ray, UV, optical and mid-IR during their hard- intermediate- and soft-states.","page":"https://www.stsci.edu/jwst/science-execution/program-information?id=6561"},"JWST:12525":{"cat":"CAL/CROSS","aim":"This program provides observations of two stars spread throughout the year to measure the repeatability of JWST observations.","page":"https://www.stsci.edu/jwst/science-execution/program-information?id=12525"},"JWST:12772":{"cat":"ENG","aim":"Test for MIRI moving target","page":"https://www.stsci.edu/jwst/science-execution/program-information?id=12772"},"JWST:9579":{"cat":"GO","aim":"To find this population of planets, we propose a multi-cycle, common proper motion survey in the mid-infrared to find and confirm an unexplored population of giant planets around 30 nearby white dwarf stars at orbital separations ranging from 4 to 500 au.","page":"https://www.stsci.edu/jwst/science-execution/program-information?id=9579"},"JWST:12596":{"cat":"CAL/MIRI","aim":"The MIRI 4QPM coronagraphs require the target to be centered on the nulling position to better than 10 mas.","page":"https://www.stsci.edu/jwst/science-execution/program-information?id=12596"}}}